WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.640
Today we are going to look at a&nbsp;
paper with two twists. You know what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:19.280
I’ll give you twist number one right away. This&nbsp;
human isn’t here. This human isn’t here either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.640
And, neither is this human here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.400
Now, you are probably asking Károly,&nbsp;
what are you even talking about?

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.600
Now hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
because I am talking about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:46.800
Look. This is the geometry of this virtual&nbsp;
human inside a virtual world. Whoa. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.520
all of these people are in a synthetic video, and&nbsp;
in a virtual environment that can be changed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:01.760
a simple click. And more importantly, as we change&nbsp;
the lighting or the environment, it also simulates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.960
the effect of that environment on the character,&nbsp;
making it look like they are really there.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.720
So that sounds good, but how do we take&nbsp;
a human and make a digital copy of them?

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.520
Well, first, we place them in a capture&nbsp;
system that contains hundreds of led lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:27.200
and an elaborate sensor for capturing&nbsp;
depth information. Why do we need these?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.720
Well, all this this gives the system plenty&nbsp;
of data on how the skin, hair and the clothes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:42.640
reflect light. And, at this point, we know&nbsp;
everything we need to know and can now proceed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.080
and place our virtual copy in a computer&nbsp;
game or even a telepresence meeting.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.960
Now, this is already amazing, but two things&nbsp;
really stick out here. One you will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.960
when you look at this previous competing work.&nbsp;
This had a really smooth output geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.720
which means that only few high-frequency&nbsp;
details were retained. This other work was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:15.920
better at retaining the details, but, look, tons&nbsp;
of artifacts appear when the model is moving.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.280
And, what does the new one look like?&nbsp;
Is it any better? Let’s have a look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.800
Oh my! We get tons of fine details, and&nbsp;
the movements have improved significantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.360
Not perfect by any means, look here,&nbsp;
but still, an amazing leap forward.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.120
Two, the other remarkable thing here&nbsp;
is that the results are so realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.840
that objects the in virtual scene can cast a&nbsp;
shadow on our model. What a time to be alive!

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:58.800
And now, yes, you remember that I promised&nbsp;
two twists. Where is twist number two? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:06.480
it has been here all along for the entirety of&nbsp;
this video. Have you noticed? Look, all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:13.680
is from 2019. From two years ago. Two years is&nbsp;
a long time in machine learning and computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.640
graphics research, and I cannot wait to see how it&nbsp;
will be improved two more papers down the line. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.400
you are excited too, make sure to subscribe and&nbsp;
hit the bell icon to not miss it when it appears.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

